(45,'PLO 1','Apply knowledge of computing fundamentals, technical concepts and models, standards, business processes, mathematics, and social science, and domain knowledge appropriate for the information systems practice to the abstraction and conceptualization of solution models from defined problems and requirements'),
(45,'PLO 10','Recognize the need, and have the ability, to engage in independent learning for continual development as an Information Systems professional.'),
(45,'PLO 2','Identify, formulate, research literature, and analyze user needs and taking them into account to solve complex information systems problems, reaching substantiated conclusions using fundamental principles of mathematics, computing fundamentals, technical concepts and standards in the core information systems, and relevant domain disciplines.'),
(45,'PLO 3','Evaluate information systems in terms of general quality attributes and possible trade-offs presented within the given requirement'),
(45,'PLO 4','Model, design and evaluate systems, components, and/or processes as possible solutions for complex information systems problems, to meet specified user and/or organizational needs, goals and constraints taking into account cost-benefit efficiency with appropriate societal and'),
(45,'PLO 5','Create, select, adapt, use appropriate techniques, methods, resources, and modern computing tools to complex problem solving and decision-making activities, with an understanding of the limitations'),
(45,'PLO 6','Function effectively as an individual and as a member or leader in diverse teams and in multidisciplinary settings through collaborative processes'),
(45,'PLO 7','Communicate effectively with the computing community and with society at large about (in local and international scenes) complex computing activities by being able to comprehend and write effective reports, design documentation, make effective presentations, negotiate and give and understand clear instructions'),
(45,'PLO 8','Understand and assess societal, health, safety, legal, professional, ethical and cultural issues within local and global contexts, and the consequential responsibilities relevant to professional information systems practice'),
(45,'PLO 9','Understand and commit to professional ethics, responsibilities, and norms of professional computing practice, and the Lasallian core values'),
(46,'PLO 1','Apply knowledge of computing, mathematics, science, and domain knowledge appropriate for information technology practice to the abstraction and conceptualization of solution models from defined problems and requirements.'),
(46,'PLO 10','Critically reflect, understand and assess problems, issues and consequential responsibilities relevant to the professional computing practice towards a better understanding and shaping of ideas and solutions. These include societal, health, safety, legal, secular and cultural issues within local and global contexts.'),
(46,'PLO 11','Understand and commit to professional ethics, the Lasallian core values, professional responsibilities, and norms of professional computing practice, while developing awareness of the spiritual nature and religious significance of life.'),
(46,'PLO 12','Crtically evaluates and reflects on own assumptions and values, thinking, performance, behaviour and well-baing. As a result of this evaluation and reflection, the student recognizes the need and able to plan, organize, manage, and engage in independent learning for continual development as an Information Technology professional.'),
(46,'PLO 2','Apply best practices and standards in information technology.'),
(46,'PLO 3','Analyze complex problems, and identify and define computing requirements appropriate for its solution using fundamental principles of mathematics, computing fundamentals, technical concepts and practices in the core information technologies, and relevant domain disciplines.'),
(46,'PLO 4','Identify and analyze user needs and taking them into account to solve complex information technology problems, reaching substantiated conclusions using fundamental principles of mathematics, computing fundamentals, technical concepts and practices in the core information technologies, and relevant domain disciplines.'),
(46,'PLO 5','Create, select, adapt, use and apply appropriate techniques, innovative methods, resources, and modern computing tools and technologies to complex computing activities involve in solving problems, making decisions, and planning for a better future, with an undertanding of their limitations.'),
(46,'PLO 6','Design, implement, and evaluate possible solutions for complex information technology problems'),
(46,'PLO 7','Integrate and administer systems that achieve their purpose and are appropriate for their intended users and the users'' environment, while adhering to time constraints.'),
(46,'PLO 8','Function effectively as a Filipino Lasallian individual, a member or a leader in diverse teams and in multidisciplinary and multicultural settings, in developing and contributing positively to the accomplishment of team goals through collaborative processes. In order to do this, the student develops and practices Christian leadership and effective interpersonal skills to relate harmoniously with others, and evaluate their sense of responsibility through participation in a range of learning contexts.'),
(46,'PLO 9','Communicate effectively and confidently to different audiences (including peers, the computing community and with society at large; in local and international scenes) about complex computing activities by being able to comprehend and compose a range of written, spoken and visual forms of conveying information that is meaningful to society and the Church. Listening actively to the intent and spirit of others’ words, exploring ideas critically, communicating personal understanding and meaning, and expressing responses and findings based on multiple experiences, ideas, thoughts, feelings, values and understandings, clearly in verbal and non-verbal forms for a variety of purposes through peaceful, respectful, compassionate and non-discriminatory ways.'),
(50,'PLO 1','Able to apply knowledge of computing fundamentals, various algorithmic principles, computer science theories, technical concepts and practices, best practices and standards in systems development within a specific application environment, mathematics, science, and domain knowledge appropriate for the computer science practice to the modeling, design, abstraction and conceptualization of computer-based solution models from defined problems and requirements.'),
(50,'PLO 10','Critically evaluate and reflect on own assumptions and values, thinking, performance, behavior and well-being. As a result of this evaluation and reflection, student recognizes the need and is able to plan, organize, manage and engage in independent and advanced learning for continual development and growth as a Computer Science professional, as well as developing one’s awareness of the spiritual nature of life.'),
(50,'PLO 2','Identify, formulate, research literature, and analyze user or domain needs and taking them into account to solve complex computing problems, reaching substantiated conclusions using fundamental principles of mathematics, algorithmic principles, computer science theories, computing fundamentals, technical concepts and practices in systems development, and relevant domain disciplines.'),
(50,'PLO 3','Adapt and use methods, resources and modern computing tools and technologies, and innovate the application of these to complex computing activities involved in problem-solving and decision-making; with an understanding of their limitations.'),
(50,'PLO 4','Design and evaluate possible solutions for complex computing problems, and design and evaluate systems of varying levels of complexities, components, or computing processes that meet specified user and domain needs, taking into account tradeoffs involved in design choices with appropriate considerations for public health and safety, cultural, societal, environmental, and intellectual property issues. Such design and evaluation should be aligned with Christian principles and conducted with resourcefulness, creativity, insight, originality, aesthetic and moral judgment, enterprise and a risk-taking approach to meet the current and emerging needs of the diverse stakeholders.'),
(50,'PLO 5','Create products, introduce innovation, and/or define processes that achieve their purpose and are appropriate for their intended users and the users'' environment.'),
(50,'PLO 6','Function effectively and responsibly as a Lasallian individual, a member or leader in diverse teams and in multidisciplinary and multicultural settings, in developing and contributing positively to the accomplishment of team goals through leadership and collaborative processes. In order to do this, the student develops and practices Christian leadership and effective interpersonal skills to relate harmoniously to others and evaluate their values and sense of responsibility through productive participation and adaptation in a range of learning contexts.'),
(50,'PLO 7','Communicate effectively and confidently to different audiences (including peers, the computing community and with society at large; in local and international scenes) about complex computing activities by being able to comprehend and compose a range of written, spoken and visual forms of conveying information that is meaningful to society and the Church. Listening actively to the intent and spirit of others’ words, exploring ideas critically, communicating personal understanding and meaning, and expressing responses and findings based on multiple experiences, ideas, thoughts, feelings, values and understandings, clearly in verbal and non-verbal forms for a variety of purposes through peaceful, respectful, compassionate and non-discriminatory ways.'),
(50,'PLO 8','Critically reflect, understand and assess problems, issues and consequential responsibilities relevant to the professional computing practice towards a better understanding and shaping of ideas and solutions. These include societal, health, safety, legal, cultural and environmental issues within local and global contexts.'),
(50,'PLO 9','Understand and commit to professional ethics, the Lasallian core values, professional responsibilities, and norms of professional computing practice.'),
(58,'PLO 1','Explain classical, neo-classical and modern/ contemporary theories in behavioral sciences.'),
(58,'PLO 2','Conduct quantitative, qualitative and mixed social research demonstrating a thorough knowledge of local, national, and global social issues, emphasizing social groups, organizations, institutions, systems, social change, and program development.'),
